**File:** `core/src/main/java/io/kestra/core/utils/TruthUtils.java`, **line 11**
```java
public static boolean isFalsy(String condition) {
    return condition != null && FALSE_VALUES.contains(condition);
}
```

This returns `false` when `condition` is `null`. That means `null` is **not** falsy — implying `null` is truthy. This is almost certainly the wrong semantic: `null` should be falsy in any reasonable boolean-coercion system, and it is inconsistent with how most languages/tools treat `null`/`nil`/`undefined`. The `Assert` plugin calls `TruthUtils.isFalsy(renderer)` after rendering, so a condition that renders to `null` will be treated as **passing**, which is a wrong-result / silent assertion bypass.

